You can learn more at revenuecat dot com dot Let's get into the show.

6
00:00:40,880 --> 00:00:42,720
Hello, I'm your host, David Bernard.

7
00:00:43,120 --> 00:00:48,720
Today's conversation is shorter than usual and will be featured in RevenueCat State of Subscription Apps report.

8
00:00:49,600 --> 00:00:56,800
Each episode in this series will explore one crucial metric and share actionable insights from top subscription app operators.

9
00:00:57,600 --> 00:01:02,000
With me today, Brandon Gador, product lead for growth at OnX Maps.

10
00:01:02,560 --> 00:01:16,320
On the podcast, I talk with Brandon about why product is the key to monetization, the psychology of value perception, and the often overlooked impact of simply reminding users that they're on the free plan.

11
00:01:17,280 --> 00:01:19,680
Hey Brandon, thanks for joining me on the podcast today.

12
00:01:20,160 --> 00:01:20,560
Awesome.

13
00:01:20,720 --> 00:01:21,760
Thanks, Dave, for having me.

14
00:01:22,560 --> 00:01:24,960
So I'm really looking forward to chatting with you.

15
00:01:25,200 --> 00:01:29,120
I think a lot of folks in the subscription app industry don't know about Onyx.

16
00:01:29,280 --> 00:01:31,200
It's kind of this sleeping giant.

17
00:01:31,760 --> 00:01:37,920
You know, not as many folks in Silicon Valley or just in Tech Broadly are into hunting and fishing and outdoor hobbies.

18
00:01:38,400 --> 00:01:42,640
And so they just don't realize what a great business Onyx is.

19
00:01:42,880 --> 00:01:44,720
400 employees, millions of users.

20
00:01:45,200 --> 00:01:46,560
It's a pretty amazing app.

21
00:01:47,040 --> 00:01:56,640
And the thing I wanted to focus on on today was increasing monetization through a combination of targeted upsells of premium features.

22
00:01:57,040 --> 00:01:59,600
And then I do want to dig into also subscription levels.

23
00:01:59,920 --> 00:02:01,280
Let's get that first out of the way.

24
00:02:01,840 --> 00:02:07,840
How do you think about targeting users with that right upsell with the right feature to improve conversion?

25
00:02:08,400 --> 00:02:13,920
The most important thing, and this is like true for any product, is like the product really has to be solving a problem first.

26
00:02:14,480 --> 00:02:21,040
And so you can't bring monetization in if there isn't like value that the user is finding that they're willing to pay for.

27
00:02:21,200 --> 00:02:24,320
So I think like before you can even start thinking about monetization and those elements.

28
00:02:24,720 --> 00:02:31,760
Like the product market fit and is this solving a problem that users, you know, deeply care about that they're willing to pay for is the most important thing.

29
00:02:32,080 --> 00:02:38,640
I think it's just important to start there when you're thinking about monetization, it's like the product market fit and is this product really solving a problem.

30
00:02:39,200 --> 00:02:52,080
And for folks who are struggling with monetization, that's really genuinely like the first troubleshooting step is like understanding with users, is this a product solving a problem or at least something people are willing to pay for?

31
00:02:52,240 --> 00:03:01,360
So if you build a gimmick, like, yeah, maybe you're going to monetize a little bit, but there's a lot of product problems that are upstream of monetization problems.

32
00:03:01,440 --> 00:03:03,200
So I think it's a fantastic place to start.

33
00:03:03,600 --> 00:03:11,040
Like the next layer to that is really thinking about like, well, what is the monetization strategy and having kind of a clear line of sight of that.

34
00:03:11,200 --> 00:03:17,440
And so, you know, is it a trial type app where you're really trying to drive users into trials to experience the value of a product?

35
00:03:18,160 --> 00:03:23,280
Or is it more of a freemium approach where you're just trying to gobble up a bunch of free users and then convert them over time?

36
00:03:23,520 --> 00:03:24,000
And I think like...

37
00:03:24,720 --> 00:03:38,080
having a clear strategy there really informs a lot of kind of like the following motions and the metrics you start to optimize and so those two elements are like what i would you know make sure that are solid first before then you start diving into some of them the monetization pieces.

38
00:03:38,640 --> 00:03:56,960
On freemium versus free trials, I know within Onyx Maps, which is actually kind of unique in the industry, you all have three or four different products and some of those products have different strategies around whether you are just gobbling up users with the freemium strategy or doing the free trial strategy.

39
00:03:57,120 --> 00:04:03,680
And so you're kind of in a unique position working at a business that itself operates in multiple models.

40
00:04:03,840 --> 00:04:09,360
So I'd love your insights on why freemium or why Onyx chose it for the different products.

41
00:04:09,680 --> 00:04:13,360
What's really unique about our growth team at Onyx is that we're a horizontal team.

42
00:04:13,600 --> 00:04:17,760
And so we actually work across all these apps that are at different stages of their life.

43
00:04:18,000 --> 00:04:20,080
And so the Hunt app is the most mature.

44
00:04:20,240 --> 00:04:21,200
It's been around the longest.

45
00:04:21,760 --> 00:04:25,760
And then the Backcountry app is actually the newer of the apps that we have.

46
00:04:26,240 --> 00:04:31,520
And then even within that, so you have this maturity stage of the business and like the brand awareness.

47
00:04:31,680 --> 00:04:33,520
And so that definitely plays a factor in it.

48
00:04:34,080 --> 00:04:37,600
But then you also have like, well, what is the competitive landscape in these different areas?

49
00:04:37,920 --> 00:04:46,640
And in the Hunt space, the competition is radically different than in the Backcountry space where you have a much more, you know, I'd say a lot more freemium type products.

50
00:04:47,040 --> 00:04:55,440
You have more kind of casual use as far as if you think about all trails or Strava for instance where there's more repeat usage over time or like hunting is very seasonal.

51
00:04:55,680 --> 00:05:04,640
There's specific seasons when you can go out and get out in the woods and so that really plays into the strategy around how we think about what we're optimizing for when it comes to monetization.

52
00:05:04,960 --> 00:05:12,560
So then where does the rubber meet the road on converting those users and how do you think about that next piece of monetization?

53
00:05:13,040 --> 00:05:22,720
First and foremost, you need to be educating users that what is part of the paid tier, like, you know, a big thing learning that we've had is that users didn't even realize that they were in the free experience.

54
00:05:23,040 --> 00:05:47,760
And so sometimes you just need to be mega like really obvious that like hey you're part of a free product here and here's kind of the value that you're missing behind the paywall and so i think education is is a really is like the first motion i'd say and that's education around the paid tiers but then even around the features themselves and so some you know a thing we've been experimenting a lot with is just like education within the features, trying to communicate the value to the user.

55
00:05:47,920 --> 00:05:53,120
What are the outcomes that we can drive for you as the user by upgrading into one of our paid tiers?

56
00:05:53,440 --> 00:05:54,880
That's a great place to start.

57
00:05:55,280 --> 00:06:10,800
And so how are you playing with what features that users have access to over time and really trying to understand what is the particular feature or the user outcome that is most going to drive users to want to hold out their wallet and upgrade into one of the paid tiers and really experience the value of the product.

58
00:06:11,040 --> 00:06:22,560
And so playing with the access that users have to some of these features and be willing to experiment with that because I think you can have an idea or a strategy up front around this is the basket of things in our paid tier.

59
00:06:23,600 --> 00:06:26,160
But I think you'll probably learn a ton if you start experimenting with that.

60
00:06:26,800 --> 00:06:31,600
Getting that freemium mix right is what can really drive a product forward.

61
00:06:31,760 --> 00:06:44,960
So I think it's kind of an underexperimented with aspect of freemium subscriptiontion apps is actually moving features in and out of that premium experience to figure out what's retaining people and what's actually driving monetization.

62
00:06:45,040 --> 00:06:46,080
It's a fantastic tip.

63
00:06:46,480 --> 00:06:59,760
The previous podcast of yours that I love is the Headspace podcast and they talk about their journey down this path and I reference that podcast all the time because I think it's a really fascinating case study of how they played with access over time around their free user base.

64
00:07:00,080 --> 00:07:07,360
And when it comes to monetization, like, I think the third area I'd consider is, you know, you have these upsells, you're trying to drive traffic to a particular paywall.

65
00:07:07,840 --> 00:07:16,080
Now you're at paywall, you know, optimization and I think that's another really critical area for teams to think about as far as like the traffic that you're driving.

66
00:07:16,560 --> 00:07:21,200
I know in your podcast you often talk a lot about the paywall view rate and really increasing that.

67
00:07:21,360 --> 00:07:25,200
And that's like one of those levers to really think about is like take freemium for instance.

68
00:07:25,360 --> 00:07:43,360
Like how often are your free users actually seeing the paywall and what are the motions you can do to increase the paywall view rate as well as then like setting up capabilities so that you can run a lot of experiments, you know, pending volume, but like run a lot of experiments on that actual paywall and really, you know, optimize that.

69
00:07:43,840 --> 00:07:48,480
If you think about the things that we've been talking about like education, feature access into the paywall.

70
00:07:48,960 --> 00:07:52,800
and like what is that end-to-end journey for the user and how are you connecting all those things?

71
00:07:53,200 --> 00:07:56,880
There's some really interesting things to experiment and explore in those themes.

72
00:07:57,360 --> 00:08:05,840
I actually read recently that Duolingo is getting some backlash from free users because they are getting more and more aggressive about pushing people to the subscription.

73
00:08:06,320 --> 00:08:16,160
So how do you think about balancing the trade-off of like annoying the free users and potentially causing churn, but then, hey, it's a business and we do need to monetize.

74
00:08:16,560 --> 00:08:18,880
It goes back to the monetization strategy.

75
00:08:19,360 --> 00:08:31,680
And I think like if you are, let's say you're deploying a freemium strategy and you want to have this large pool of free users over time, well, you're going to think about different things if it's like really a trial-based product and we just want people to take the trial.

76
00:08:32,080 --> 00:08:37,440
Another thing that kind of helps distinguish on that strategy is like the TAM, like the overall opportunity.

77
00:08:38,000 --> 00:08:44,800
If there's a really large TAM, like you mentioned, like Duolingo, you can afford to probably have, you know, millions and millions of free users.

78
00:08:45,120 --> 00:08:47,200
Whereas for some of our products, it's more niche.

79
00:08:47,360 --> 00:08:55,360
And so, like, you know, specifically like in Hunt, it's much more of a trial strategy because that TAM isn't like, you know, everyone out trying to learn a new language.

80
00:08:55,840 --> 00:09:01,920
Another thing that's helpful to get aligned as a team when you're working in this area is like, well, what are the metricss that we're optimizing for?

81
00:09:02,160 --> 00:09:10,240
And then how do we think about the guardrail metrics of if you want to be mindful of not pushing people away, how are you thinking about repeat usage?

82
00:09:10,480 --> 00:09:11,920
How are you thinking about uninstalls?

83
00:09:12,480 --> 00:09:16,800
Are you monitoring tickets into your customer support team, app store reviews?

84
00:09:17,120 --> 00:09:20,240
How do you get that holistic view of the user experience so that you are?

85
00:09:20,800 --> 00:09:26,320
Because at the end of the day, like I mentioned earlier, we had to communicate a lot to our users that you actually weren't a free product.

86
00:09:26,640 --> 00:09:28,960
And sometimes, like, you have to get in front of them.

87
00:09:29,280 --> 00:09:30,640
And it is going to be disrupting.

88
00:09:30,880 --> 00:09:35,600
But I think in that case, like, it was important to educate our users that you're actually missing out on all this value.

89
00:09:35,680 --> 00:09:42,800
One of the things that I think onX does really well is that you are monetizing those free users well.

90
00:09:43,120 --> 00:09:46,400
And then as a more mature product, you do now have multiple tiers.

91
00:09:46,720 --> 00:10:00,320
How do you effectively use that to kind of target the less expensive tier in certain cases where maybe you know they're not going to get the full value, but then those like super diehards, you want to make sure that they can pay you as much as they're willing to pay.

92
00:10:00,960 --> 00:10:10,400
My advice for the app builders out there is like, until you really have a clear user need around an additional tier, when is the opportunity to bring it in?

93
00:10:10,560 --> 00:10:16,000
Because I think like by introducing it too earlyly, it introduces all sorts of complexity into the business.

94
00:10:16,960 --> 00:10:23,440
And it ends up being, I think, more work than it actually is worth when you have a really clear, hey, this is our paid tier.

95
00:10:23,920 --> 00:10:25,200
You have different durations of it.

96
00:10:25,760 --> 00:10:30,080
And that becomes a really easy choice for the user to understand around, like, what are they paying for?

97
00:10:30,240 --> 00:10:38,880
And so what really works well with the Hunt tiering package is that it's baked in a user problem around the access that you get across the country.

98
00:10:39,280 --> 00:10:42,960
So we have our premium product, which is one state, but we also have a premium two state package.

99
00:10:43,200 --> 00:10:48,480
And then we have our top tier, which is elite, which has access to all fifty states plus Canada and Mexico.

100
00:10:49,120 --> 00:10:59,200
I think from the user's perspective, it's really easy for them, you know, coming in onboarding, seeing the paywall, they can grock like, okay, I can understand the value or the difference between these different tiers.

101
00:10:59,920 --> 00:11:10,720
Whereas if it's just kind of a basket of features in the different tiers and you've kind of sliced and diced it by like an idea that you had, I think it's really tough for the user to understand what's the difference between these two and the value within them.

102
00:11:11,200 --> 00:11:20,960
In that elite tier, I know you all have created some partnerships and added a lot of value, like discounts to other brands and things like that that are actually really attractive.

103
00:11:21,280 --> 00:11:31,920
And again, something that Onyx can do as a much more mature product as a bigger company, but this is the kind of thing I think a lot of people can be thinking about when you think about how do we add value?

104
00:11:32,160 --> 00:11:35,440
Well, adding value isn't just that next feature you build.

105
00:11:36,240 --> 00:11:39,040
You can add value through partnerships and through so many other ways.

106
00:11:39,200 --> 00:11:40,800
So tell me how onX does that.

107
00:11:41,360 --> 00:11:44,640
This is a great point around how we think about like the elite tier.

108
00:11:44,800 --> 00:11:53,120
And there's like there's been a constant effort around how do we round out the feature set and the value that we could provide our users within our highest paying tier.

109
00:11:53,680 --> 00:11:58,800
And so, you know, our elite price is $100, which is a lot to ask for in the app store.

110
00:11:59,280 --> 00:12:00,640
Which is funny because I kind of.

111
00:12:01,200 --> 00:12:14,320
don't think it is in a way because I think you'll still have a ton of room to grow monetization because I know a lot of hunters here in the Texas area and I mean you'll spend thousands if not tens of thousands of dollars a year on this hobby.

112
00:12:14,960 --> 00:12:22,480
So yes, for an app it's expensive broadly probably not exactly and I think it's in the context of the user and like what they're spending for their hobby.

113
00:12:22,640 --> 00:12:23,520
So you're totally right.

114
00:12:23,920 --> 00:12:29,280
But I think in the mindset of like paying, you're in the app store and you're paying like 100 dollars is a lot at the time.

115
00:12:29,600 --> 00:12:36,720
And so a part of that strategy has been like how do we broaden the feature set and capabilities in our elite package and one component of that is the partnership.

116
00:12:36,720 --> 00:12:37,760
And part of that is the partnerships with brands.

117
00:12:38,000 --> 00:12:44,800
And so as an elite member, you get a discount or some sort of like extra value with these partners that we have.

118
00:12:45,200 --> 00:12:52,320
And to your point, these hobbies are really expensive and sometimes the deals that you can get on these partners can make up the value of the app in a single purchase.

119
00:12:52,480 --> 00:12:53,280
So you're exactly right.

120
00:12:53,760 --> 00:12:56,080
Do those partnerships also play a growth at all?

121
00:12:56,240 --> 00:13:02,880
Like when you partner with an outdoor clothing line like camo and all that kind of stuff, do they help promote the app?

122
00:13:03,360 --> 00:13:03,840
Yeah, exactly.

123
00:13:04,080 --> 00:13:08,160
To give you like an example, in the HUD product, we partner with First Light.

124
00:13:08,400 --> 00:13:11,040
And so First Light is an outdoor apparel company.

125
00:13:11,360 --> 00:13:23,840
And so there's a lot of kind of of cross promotion that happens between those two brands that, um, you know, first like being a premier outdoor apparel company, when people see onix next to that, um, that association happens there.

126
00:13:24,000 --> 00:13:27,360
And so we do a lot of kind of cross collaboration with these brands to help drive growth.

127
00:13:27,840 --> 00:13:40,880
What a great win-win where you're able to deliver value to your users and and create those extra incentives for the premium tier, but then you're also driving some extra growth and brand awareness through these partnerships.

128
00:13:41,120 --> 00:13:46,400
Any other specific examples around targeted upsells that you think the audience would be interested in?

129
00:13:46,880 --> 00:13:48,480
One of the areas where we probably.

130
00:13:48,560 --> 00:13:56,960
We probably learned the most around these targeted upsells and kind of to your previous question around like how do we find that balance of not being intrusive in the experiences?

131
00:13:57,600 --> 00:14:00,480
Well, one is like how do you bake it natively into the experience?

132
00:14:00,720 --> 00:14:08,960
And like anytime you can naturally have that upgrade flow or that CTA as part of like the natural user experience, like that's where we've seen success.

133
00:14:09,200 --> 00:14:12,480
And as far as the user experience, because you're not, it's not like a jarring interruption.

134
00:14:12,960 --> 00:14:18,480
But I'd say another like really critical part of this is like the strategy is like the who and when.

135
00:14:19,040 --> 00:14:30,960
And so who are the cohorts that you're targeting and being really thoughtful around the specific cohorts and being able to target on different vectors, but then the when and the context and like how you think about the context of the user.

136
00:14:31,200 --> 00:14:33,200
Did they recently interact with a particular feature?

137
00:14:33,440 --> 00:14:34,800
How long have they been with the product?

138
00:14:35,600 --> 00:14:37,440
And I'd say it takes both of those.

139
00:14:37,840 --> 00:14:42,400
You need the who and the context to really be working together to be most impactful here.

140
00:14:42,640 --> 00:14:53,200
I think the last part of that is kind of how do you carry that through into the experience of the upgrade and the context of the value that you're communicating to the user when you've got them to kind of start that journey of the upgrade path.

141
00:14:53,760 --> 00:15:00,640
And I think all trails does a really good job with this with some of their upsells where, for instance, like if you're a free user and you interact with the three D part of their map.

142
00:15:01,040 --> 00:15:06,800
Now the paywall actually has this great highlight video of 3D and it kind of is it's giving you the value prop of 3D.

143
00:15:07,360 --> 00:15:15,920
And so it kind of goes back to that and then user experience I was referring to earlier is like how are you having that continuity through the entire upgrade is where you'll see the most impact.

144
00:15:16,320 --> 00:15:17,760
This has been so much fun, Brandon.

145
00:15:18,000 --> 00:15:20,240
I think there's so many tips folks can take away.

146
00:15:20,560 --> 00:15:24,800
Anything you wanted to share as we're wrapping up is the team hiring or anything else you wanted to share?

147
00:15:25,360 --> 00:15:28,560
No, I mean, first off, thank you, David, so much for having me on the podcast.

148
00:15:28,960 --> 00:15:33,360
I'm a huge fan of the work that you all are doing and I've learned a ton from the different guests that you've had.

149
00:15:33,680 --> 00:15:34,960
So I really appreciate everything you do there.

150
00:15:35,040 --> 00:15:41,120
I mean, the last thing I'd say is like, you know, Onyx, these are purpose-built products for folks that are trying to get out in the outdoors.

151
00:15:41,280 --> 00:15:47,440
And so whether you're a backcountry skier, a hiker, an off-roader, or you're curious about hunting, you know, check out some of our apps.

152
00:15:47,520 --> 00:15:48,640
They're pretty amazing products.

153
00:15:48,800 --> 00:15:49,440
And yeah.

154
00:15:49,680 --> 00:15:50,080
Awesome.

155
00:15:50,160 --> 00:15:51,360
Thanks so much for joining me today.

156
00:15:51,440 --> 00:15:52,080
This is so much fun.

157
00:15:52,320 --> 00:15:52,960
Thanks, Damon.

158
00:15:55,520 --> 00:15:56,800
Thanks so much for listening.

159
00:15:57,760 --> 00:16:01,120
If you have a minute, please leave a review in your favorite podcast player.

